course: dictionary
lesson: 3251
title: Epileptic and the brain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Epileptic is an adjective. It means to do with epilepsy, a condition of the brain that can cause seizures, also called fits. «In Cardiff, Dafydd's brother has an epileptic seizure at the park.» Here epileptic tells you the seizure is linked to epilepsy.
explain: Context helps again. In a seizure a person may shake, stare blankly or fall. Those clues show the word is about the body and the brain, not about a place or a sport. Look for the clues around a word and they will often tell you what kind of word it is.
explain: Words about people need care. Many people now say «a person with epilepsy» rather than «an epileptic», because it puts the person first. Choosing kind words is part of being a good friend and a good writer.
fact: Epilepsy is a condition of the brain.
fact: A seizure, also called a fit, is an episode when a person may shake, stare blankly or fall.
fact: «Epileptic» means to do with epilepsy.
fact: If someone has a seizure, keep calm, move hard things away and cushion their head. Do not hold them down or put anything in their mouth.
fact: Time the seizure and call an adult. If it lasts a long time, call emergency services: 999, 911, 000 or 111, depending on where you live.
exercise: «In Cardiff, Dafydd's brother has an epileptic seizure at the park.» In this sentence, epileptic means | To do with the weather | To do with the park | *To do with epilepsy, a condition of the brain | A kind of fast running
exercise: A seizure, or fit, can make a person do what? | *Shake, stare blankly or fall | Sing loudly and dance | Swim fast in a pool | Read a book quietly
exercise: Which is the kinder way to talk about a person with epilepsy? | That epileptic kid | The epileptic one | An epileptic | *A person with epilepsy
test: If someone has a seizure, what should you do first? | Run away and tell nobody | Put a spoon in their mouth | *Keep calm and move hard things away | Hold them tightly still
test: Epileptic is which kind of word? | A number | A verb | *An adjective | A place
exam: When should you call for help during a seizure? | *If it lasts a long time | Only if the person asks you to | Never, even if it lasts a long time | Only after it has been over for a day
